.TH install 1 (date) "GNU coreutils (unreleased)"
.SH NAME
install \- copy files and set attributes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY install
.RI [ OPTION \~.\|.\|.\&]
.RB [ \-T ]
.I SOURCE
.I DEST
.YS
.SY install
.RI [ OPTION \~.\|.\|.\&]
.IR SOURCE \~.\|.\|.\&
.I DIRECTORY
.YS
.SY install
.RI [ OPTION \~.\|.\|.\&]
.BI \-t\~ DIRECTORY
.IR SOURCE \~.\|.\|.\&
.YS
.SY install
.RI [ OPTION \~.\|.\|.\&]
.BI \-d
.IR DIRECTORY \~.\|.\|.\&
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.\" Add any additional description here
.PP
This install program copies files (often just compiled) into destination
locations you choose.  If you want to download and install a ready\-to\-use
package on a GNU/Linux system, you should instead be using a package manager
like \fByum\fP(1) or \fBapt\-get\fP(1).
.PP
In the first three forms, copy SOURCE to DEST or multiple SOURCE(s) to
the existing DIRECTORY, while setting permission modes and owner/group.
In the 4th form, create all components of the given DIRECTORY(ies).
.PP
Mandatory arguments to long options are mandatory for short options too.
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-\-backup
\fB\-\-backup[=CONTROL]\fP
.UE
make a backup of each existing destination file
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-b
\fB\-b\fP
.UE
like \fB\-\-backup\fR but does not accept an argument
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-c
\fB\-c\fP
.UE
(ignored)
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-C
\fB\-C, \-\-compare\fP
.UE
compare content of source and destination files,
and if no change to content, ownership, and permissions,
do not modify the destination at all
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-d
\fB\-d, \-\-directory\fP
.UE
treat all arguments as directory names;
create all components of the specified directories
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-D
\fB\-D\fP
.UE
create all leading components of DEST except the last,
or all components of \fB\-\-target\-directory\fR,
then copy SOURCE to DEST
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-\-debug
\fB\-\-debug\fP
.UE
explain how a file is copied.  Implies \fB\-v\fR
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-g
\fB\-g, \-\-group=\,GROUP\fP
.UE
set group ownership, instead of process' current group
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-m
\fB\-m, \-\-mode=\,MODE\fP
.UE
set permission mode (as in chmod), instead of rwxr\-xr\-x
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-o
\fB\-o, \-\-owner=\,OWNER\fP
.UE
set ownership (super\-user only)
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-p
\fB\-p, \-\-preserve\-timestamps\fP
.UE
apply access/modification times of SOURCE files
to corresponding destination files
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-s
\fB\-s, \-\-strip\fP
.UE
strip symbol tables
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-\-strip\-program
\fB\-\-strip\-program=PROGRAM\fP
.UE
program used to strip binaries
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-S
\fB\-S, \-\-suffix=\,SUFFIX\fP
.UE
override the usual backup suffix
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-t
\fB\-t, \-\-target\-directory=\,DIRECTORY\fP
.UE
copy all SOURCE arguments into DIRECTORY
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-T
\fB\-T, \-\-no\-target\-directory\fP
.UE
treat DEST as a normal file
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-v
\fB\-v, \-\-verbose\fP
.UE
print the name of each created file or directory
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-\-preserve\-context
\fB\-\-preserve\-context\fP
.UE
preserve SELinux security context
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-Z
\fB\-Z\fP
.UE
set SELinux security context of destination file
and each created directory, to default type
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#install\-\-context
\fB\-\-context[=CTX]\fP
.UE
like \fB\-Z\fR, or if CTX is specified then set the
SELinux or SMACK security context to CTX
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/install#install\-\-help
\fB\-\-help\fP
.UE
display this help and exit
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/install#install\-\-version
\fB\-\-version\fP
.UE
output version information and exit
.PP
The backup suffix is '~', unless set with \fB\-\-suffix\fR or SIMPLE_BACKUP_SUFFIX.
The version control method may be selected via the \fB\-\-backup\fR option or through
the VERSION_CONTROL environment variable.  Here are the values:
.TP
none, off
never make backups (even if \fB\-\-backup\fR is given)
.TP
numbered, t
make numbered backups
.TP
existing, nil
numbered if numbered backups exist, simple otherwise
.TP
simple, never
always make simple backups
